{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1618580080,"post_id":41512,"comment_id":88333,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"+1. I bolded the takeaway, as it&#39;s very important to understand. (I hope that&#39;s ok. Feel free to revert if it seems too much.) Everything I&#39;ve read agrees with this (with the exception of dead time.) When I needed a break from more active parenting, my 10 month old and I would watch Sesame Street together, him on my lap, and I would narrate what was going on (&quot;Look at that pig! That&#39;s a big, happy, very muddy pig!&quot;) That was before the concept of &quot;screen time&quot;, as we didn&#39;t have computers (!!!) back then. But we did have videos, and that was counted as &#39;screen time&#39;."},{"owner":{"account_id":504225,"reputation":446,"user_id":40949,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6ca2769dc497a8c037d3c9b434d0c86e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Sean Duggan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40949/sean-duggan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1618876448,"post_id":41510,"comment_id":88344,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you. Yours was also a good answer, but the other one was earlier, and more directly to the point of what I needed."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1619078325,"post_id":41526,"comment_id":88350,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Exactly - I just didn’t dare to recommend letting baby sleep at night <i>if</i> it went against doctor‘a orders. Hence the comment asking for clarification."},{"owner":{"account_id":504225,"reputation":446,"user_id":40949,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6ca2769dc497a8c037d3c9b434d0c86e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Sean Duggan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40949/sean-duggan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619616110,"post_id":41526,"comment_id":88410,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Sorry it took me a bit to get back to this. Our doctor has gone back and forth on whether to wake him during the night, and he&#39;s gotten better at waking up from naps for feeding. We unfortunately have had to give up on breastfeeding. The spitting up has been getting a little worse lately, but I think it&#39;s in part because his appetite is outpacing his stomach size. Thank you for your answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":504225,"reputation":446,"user_id":40949,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6ca2769dc497a8c037d3c9b434d0c86e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Sean Duggan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40949/sean-duggan"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1619756013,"post_id":41526,"comment_id":88449,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@SeanDuggan - I know there&#39;s a lot of pressure (and benefits) to breastfeed, but sometimes it just doesn&#39;t work out. Please do get some skin-to-skin time in; it doesn&#39;t matter if it&#39;s food from the breast or bottle. It will do a lot of good for bonding. Please  feel free to ask questions here whenever you have them! We&#39;ve all been through this."},{"owner":{"account_id":325145,"reputation":8805,"user_id":27868,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/rX1ss.png?s=256","display_name":"Pascal remembers Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27868/pascal-remembers-monica"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1619797986,"post_id":41567,"comment_id":88456,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi Joseph. Welcome to the site! Would you mind fleshing out your answer a bit? You&#39;re providing a link that is relevant, and there&#39;s some good advice, but you could improve it to make sure the answer is understandable and complete by itself. The link can serve as a reference for the quotes and provide more detail, but readers shouldn&#39;t be required to click on it to understand the answer. It won&#39;t take much to improve the answer - it would help greatly, for example, if you explained who Herman refers to in the second paragraph before quoting him, so people don&#39;t need to click to find out."},{"owner":{"account_id":2326492,"reputation":183,"user_id":14985,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/4c214f2154dbac02d5e7e73d0ece64c2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"bandybabboon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14985/bandybabboon"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1620769328,"post_id":41611,"comment_id":88589,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"yes hand her a list if she says the same thing too often. Nice work, good going, well done, wow great, fabulous, fantastic, super...&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":1588376,"reputation":131,"user_id":37976,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cbd59e323ca96a9b4fdf3065590ecd0e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Andrew Brēza","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37976/andrew-br%c4%93za"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1620827667,"post_id":41611,"comment_id":88615,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Another alternative would be something like &quot;You&#39;re being such a good girl!&quot; Might be a decent compromise."},{"owner":{"account_id":189496,"reputation":215,"user_id":25566,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/J477b.png?s=256","display_name":"TonyK","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25566/tonyk"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":14410,"reputation":2980,"user_id":25791,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/J38sS.jpg?s=256","display_name":"T.E.D.","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25791/t-e-d"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1620849687,"post_id":41624,"comment_id":88632,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@T.E.D. I stand by my answer. It&#39;s the only one that gets to the heart of the issue. Perhaps it might even do some good."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1620892525,"post_id":41624,"comment_id":88649,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TonyK - no. Personal attacks are not allowed, hence the edit. You may stand by the answer, but this site will not. Please read our <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/tour\">tour</a> and <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/how-to-answer\">How to Answer</a> pages again to understand how we expect members of this community to behave."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1620912557,"post_id":41613,"comment_id":88654,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Comments are not for extended discussion; this conversation has been <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/124218/discussion-on-answer-by-anongoodnurse-grandmother-keeps-calling-my-daughter-goo\">moved to chat</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1620925346,"post_id":41624,"comment_id":88656,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RoryAlsop - Neither are frame challenges unless invited by the OP."},{"owner":{"account_id":310950,"reputation":101,"user_id":41168,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/150b72ef55e91a3e1e5b4434b356c5a9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Lee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41168/lee"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1620979093,"post_id":41627,"comment_id":88660,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think this is the best answer. If there really were a problem with saying &quot;good x&quot; we&#39;d have a serious societal problem, which is not evident. Point 4 is especially important."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":310950,"reputation":101,"user_id":41168,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/150b72ef55e91a3e1e5b4434b356c5a9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Lee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41168/lee"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1621012405,"post_id":41627,"comment_id":88666,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Lee - Look around you. We don&#39;t have serious societal problems? What country do you live in? My country (the US) has more problems than sea otters have hair (100,000–400,000 hairs per square centimeter, if you care to count)!"},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1621068226,"post_id":41612,"comment_id":88678,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Comments are not for extended discussion; this conversation has been <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/124280/discussion-on-answer-by-deworde-grandmother-keeps-calling-my-daughter-good-girl\">moved to chat</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":137388,"reputation":130,"user_id":33369,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/CueIb.jpg?s=256","display_name":"J. Chris Compton","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33369/j-chris-compton"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1621606617,"post_id":41626,"comment_id":88704,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A heartfelt &quot;good girl&quot; from the grandparent would be better than a &#39;thought about&#39; other comment. Your daughter is nine months old at that point... the more people she feels loved by the better. If she were six+ years old, it is a different conversation."},{"owner":{"account_id":137388,"reputation":130,"user_id":33369,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/CueIb.jpg?s=256","display_name":"J. Chris Compton","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33369/j-chris-compton"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1621606841,"post_id":41611,"comment_id":88705,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Children do better with repetition (if you disagree look into the show Blue&#39;s Clues and why it is formatted the way it is). Pick one or two, consistency is a good thing for children under 2 years."},{"owner":{"account_id":12252380,"reputation":600,"user_id":32209,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6fec1a984ec0320a83126667eff3a0e7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"suse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32209/suse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1623172287,"post_id":41595,"comment_id":88822,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Since a baby&#39;s mouth and the breast nipple usually ( or at least they should) make quite a tight seal, excess air is less of a problem with breastfeeding than it is with bottle-feeding, where the seal is not as tight.  But recent evidence shows that burping is not science-based after all. <a href=\"https://www.sciencenews.org/blog/growth-curve/maybe-you-dont-need-burp-your-baby\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">sciencenews.org/blog/growth-curve/&hellip;</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623196972,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88826,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Some studies show that empathy starts as early as in infancy. If the child is old enough to ask for things (like &quot;more&quot;), she has a sense of self. (What do you think would happen if she asked for more [e.g. milk], and her mother served her <i>father</i> instead? Confusion indicates a clear sense of self vs. other, and crying angrily or sadly could indicate a sense of <i>right and wrong</i>. You should really back these claims up with facts (if you could find them.) -1."},{"owner":{"account_id":17207286,"reputation":1042,"user_id":39480,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f13f25a4cc7d2a267aae0ce38fc35213?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jan Niklas Fingerle","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39480/jan-niklas-fingerle"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623200280,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88830,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse It&#39;s true that some basic foundations of empathy develop or start to show early on. For the problem at hand the child has to be able to (1) differentiate between themselves and others and (2) understand that those others may experience things and feel differently from themselves."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623203114,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88831,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I read the article (which was without a single respected source) and it does not fully support your answer. Do you really believe that at age 12 mos, there is no sense of <i>me</i>?"},{"owner":{"account_id":6791317,"reputation":1624,"user_id":27624,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/eAfikIvI.png?s=256","display_name":"Luke Sawczak","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27624/luke-sawczak"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1623208638,"post_id":41692,"comment_id":88832,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Indeed, much too early to be of any concern. My grandma tells me her family only spoke Pennsylfanische Dietsch at home, and when she went to Kindergarten at a Stolzeleid (&quot;proud people&quot;, non-Mennonite) school, she was forced to learn English because there was total mutual incomprehension. So... she did. Then she became a teacher of English teachers for the Toronto District School Board! And OP&#39;s daughter has a two-year headstart on my grandma :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":17207286,"reputation":1042,"user_id":39480,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f13f25a4cc7d2a267aae0ce38fc35213?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jan Niklas Fingerle","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39480/jan-niklas-fingerle"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623220341,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88834,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse Since the article is in agreement especially with the first two book, which are well-sourced, I still find it helpful. In the context of this question I&#39;m talking about the step that the article describes at 18 months. There are developmental steps before that point, so no, I would never talk of &quot;no sense of me&quot;. But I talked about a <i>strong concept</i>, and that, in this context, would emerge later than the current age of the children. (And still remains only one of two necessary steps for them to understand the impact of their actions on others.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1623243541,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88836,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I can&#39;t think that asking a baby for permission to change a diaper is a good idea. Tell them what is happening. Say things like &quot;we are going to the supermarket now&quot;. Ask whether they want the blue coat or the red one. But asking if you can do something that is non-negotiable is just silly. They won&#39;t understand that the nappy rash has anything to do with their decision."},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1623243660,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88837,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The second paragraph (&quot;its your responsibility&quot;) is fine. The rest is irrelevant."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623248578,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88839,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@JanNiklasFingerle - Anyone  can argue about when a <i>&quot;strong sense of me&quot;</i> occurs, if there is no definition attached. Frankly, that&#39;s nonsense. An abused child/person may not have a <i>&quot;strong sense of me&quot;</i> until late in adulthood. Please define your terms and provide support from a reliable source, not an unavailable book you say you read."},{"owner":{"account_id":17207286,"reputation":1042,"user_id":39480,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f13f25a4cc7d2a267aae0ce38fc35213?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jan Niklas Fingerle","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39480/jan-niklas-fingerle"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623249006,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88841,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@PaulJohnson There is a significant number of parents in Germany, that do just that (in the &quot;Artgerecht&quot; movement). I believe they have a point that this will strengthen the baby&#39;s sense of self and personal boundaries. They also claim, this sense of &quot;I can say no&quot; might help the child later in life to assert themselves against sexual violations, and this also seems reasonable to me. But yes, it comes at the cost you mention, that&#39;s why I said that you have to be prepared to take no for an answer if you go that way."},{"owner":{"account_id":17207286,"reputation":1042,"user_id":39480,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f13f25a4cc7d2a267aae0ce38fc35213?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jan Niklas Fingerle","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39480/jan-niklas-fingerle"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623249370,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88842,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse I&#39;m not planning on turning this into a dissertation. I believe, in the context of the question the average reader will understand what I mean by &#39;strong concept of &quot;myself&quot; and &quot;other people&quot;&#39;. The books are not unavailable; I&#39;m happy to provide Amazon links, if that&#39;s of any help to you."},{"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623338613,"post_id":41692,"comment_id":88852,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;And this is preschool.&quot; In Spain they start school at age 6, so its probably &#39;real&#39; school. Your point is still valid. I started school in the Netherlands when we emigrated when I was 6. Picking up the language was painless, as is common for children that age :-)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1386584,"reputation":141,"user_id":41046,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f198c392193c117c7725831fef6f10ad?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user1318499","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41046/user1318499"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":12252380,"reputation":600,"user_id":32209,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6fec1a984ec0320a83126667eff3a0e7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"suse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32209/suse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1625463517,"post_id":41595,"comment_id":88943,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@suse With inspiration from that study, I don&#39;t burp my baby anymore and it&#39;s just fine. Sometimes she wakes needing a burp and I just pick her up and she burps automatically then goes straight back to sleep. Tedious burping session gone-burger!"},{"owner":{"account_id":16910905,"reputation":179,"user_id":41117,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/03d1e2e79bef51857e951d3ca2c80f2b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"VWFeature","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41117/vwfeature"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1625775421,"post_id":41696,"comment_id":88981,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think this was downvoted because you DIDN&#39;T ANSWER THE QUESTION of &quot;what to do?&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1627669117,"post_id":41825,"comment_id":89092,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This is good advice, but does it directly answer the question (&quot;How do I stop my two year old from hitting/kicking my five year old?&quot;)?"},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1627726980,"post_id":41825,"comment_id":89095,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Can you please reference the claim that children understand consequences from 6 months of age? That&#39;s entirely at odds with the litteratur I&#39;ve seen."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1627774024,"post_id":41827,"comment_id":89096,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Toddlers in car seats are not &quot;supposed&quot; to be in the front seat (i.e. next to the driver), as it is significantly less safe. Considering he likes to kick, it&#39;s probably much less safe for the driver as well."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1628645210,"post_id":41853,"comment_id":89169,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"+1 for this answer. On this and many other SE sites, if you state generally unrecognized things as undisputed facts, please add a source. &quot;...by the circadian rhythm or sleep system which is fully developed between the ages of 5 to 6 months&quot; is incorrect."},{"owner":{"account_id":3203153,"reputation":119,"user_id":39692,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/31xfi.jpg?s=256","display_name":"sam_smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39692/sam-smith"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629265143,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89187,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Do you think you could adjust this answer to focus more on hanging from a bar and less on swinging around? I already know swinging is not good but I don’t know about whether hanging from a bar until their grip runs out is bad and at what age"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3203153,"reputation":119,"user_id":39692,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/31xfi.jpg?s=256","display_name":"sam_smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39692/sam-smith"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629265212,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89188,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@sam_smith as I explain at the end, no. That would not be on topic, in my opinion, as you&#39;re asking if it is safe for your child to do something - we don&#39;t know your child, we can&#39;t evaluate that; ask your doctor.  The reason for &quot;do not swing your child&quot; is not because of grip - it is because of dislocating their elbow."},{"owner":{"account_id":3203153,"reputation":119,"user_id":39692,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/31xfi.jpg?s=256","display_name":"sam_smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39692/sam-smith"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629266051,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89189,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I am asking: is it safe for my child to hang from a static bar. With someone ensuring they don’t fall and hurt themselves. Your answer is saying - don’t swing them around. I know that - I posted it as the first line of the question. At the moment it doesn’t feel your answer does more than explain the links I already posted in my question."},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3203153,"reputation":119,"user_id":39692,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/31xfi.jpg?s=256","display_name":"sam_smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39692/sam-smith"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629269192,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89190,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@sam_smith, going by the information in the first link provided, Nursemaid&#39;s elbow can even occur when the child rolls onto their elbow in the wrong way. This means very little force is needed and even hanging still could be too much. But we, random strangers on the internet, cannot tell how big the risk is for <i>your</i> child."},{"owner":{"account_id":3203153,"reputation":119,"user_id":39692,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/31xfi.jpg?s=256","display_name":"sam_smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39692/sam-smith"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629272872,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89192,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"That is more of an answer right there though. Joe’s answer only talks about nursemaids elbow being cause by sharp pulling motions. It might be the answer I am looking for is looking at the issues that can occur with children’s arms and then extrapolation of hanging into those scenarios"},{"owner":{"account_id":85750,"reputation":1992,"user_id":16114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ca29123b5669b26f9b6ca2c62d9d215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Wayne Conrad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16114/wayne-conrad"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629290989,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89194,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I don&#39;t understand how addressing whether a particular activity has a risk of injury is &quot;giving medical advice.&quot;  Nobody is being told how to treat the injury."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":85750,"reputation":1992,"user_id":16114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ca29123b5669b26f9b6ca2c62d9d215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Wayne Conrad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16114/wayne-conrad"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629291122,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89195,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@WayneConrad In my opinion, it is, based on what else in the past has been seen as such; certainly welcome to disagree!"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1629328530,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89201,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe - Unless the child has previously experienced a Nursemaid&#39;s Elbow, I have <i>never</i> seen it from swinging from monkey bars. I suppose it <i>might</i> happen if they are unsupported and one hand slips, resulting in the child rotating into a pronation position. Caution is fine, but blog sites are not the right place to get  accurate info."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1629328618,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89202,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse I certainly didn&#39;t mean to imply it could - I specifically avoided answering that question entirely, solely answering the age question (as to when the risk that is <i>related to swinging/pulling by one arm</i> is largely gone).  That seems quite consistent.  In any event, I am a bit surprised to see you answer in comments here - why is this not medical advice? (I absolutely would agree this is not a risk, but avoided that side as it seemed like medical advice.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1629394674,"post_id":41868,"comment_id":89205,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe - Yeah, I know and I agree that it&#39;s medical advice, and that I should not give it, but from the beginning, I&#39;ve said I was not opposed to giving medical advice, I was opposed to <i>giving bad medical advice</i> (no implication about your answer!) I&#39;d hate to see this father miss out on a perfectly normal activity/bonding experience with his child because of an overly cautious answer. However, the site does not allow medical advice in comments, so please feel free to flag it for removal. I would not be offended in the least."},{"owner":{"account_id":10627210,"reputation":131,"user_id":41623,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0i2Id.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Artur M&#252;ller Romanov","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41623/artur-m%c3%bcller-romanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629468231,"post_id":41878,"comment_id":89208,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Yes, we understand that there must be a balance between feeling loved and learning independence. Thats the threshold we&#39;re trying to figure out right now. Another thing is that WE are starting to adapt to him. We can sleep better even with him crying at night (at least one of us). This is an amazing learning experience."},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10627210,"reputation":131,"user_id":41623,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0i2Id.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Artur M&#252;ller Romanov","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41623/artur-m%c3%bcller-romanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629468374,"post_id":41878,"comment_id":89209,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ArturM&#252;llerRomanov: Not to burst a romantic bubble, but at your baby&#39;s current age, its less about &quot;feeling loved&quot; and more about &quot;familiar sensations&quot; (it mimics being in the womb, which soothes them), and similarly, not &quot;learning independence&quot; but rather &quot;not being exposed to less ideal sleep circumstances&quot;. Your child will not feel unloved just because you put them down in a bed. They are simply not at that developmental stage yet."},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10627210,"reputation":131,"user_id":41623,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0i2Id.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Artur M&#252;ller Romanov","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41623/artur-m%c3%bcller-romanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629468702,"post_id":41878,"comment_id":89210,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ArturM&#252;llerRomanov: I would also advise to not simply ignore your crying baby. You are supposed to soothe them so that they don&#39;t cry. This is the main contributor to creating the natural instinct that Mom and Dad are a safe place and will make you feel better. If you ignore their cries, they don&#39;t learn that. That being said, if it&#39;s been hours of tending to him and him still crying anyway, and you need a short break, it&#39;s perfectly fine to put them in a safe spot and tend to yourself. Just don&#39;t ignore your baby without reason or for extended periods of time."},{"owner":{"account_id":10627210,"reputation":131,"user_id":41623,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0i2Id.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Artur M&#252;ller Romanov","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41623/artur-m%c3%bcller-romanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629469367,"post_id":41878,"comment_id":89212,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Id disagree on some points. Depriving your child to much will make it grow up an unsocial ice brick. Balance"},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10627210,"reputation":131,"user_id":41623,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0i2Id.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Artur M&#252;ller Romanov","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41623/artur-m%c3%bcller-romanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629470045,"post_id":41878,"comment_id":89213,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ArturM&#252;llerRomanov: <i>Eventually</i>, you are correct that continually depriving or avoiding interaction with a child negatively impacts them. But you&#39;re a few chapters ahead of where your child currently is. A 3 week old has no concept of social behavior, nor emotional interaction with others, nor are they receptive to training. Eventually, they will register all those things, but they&#39;re not there yet. My advice isn&#39;t intended to be applied forever. It&#39;s matched to your child&#39;s current age."},{"owner":{"account_id":10627210,"reputation":131,"user_id":41623,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0i2Id.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Artur M&#252;ller Romanov","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41623/artur-m%c3%bcller-romanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629524580,"post_id":41878,"comment_id":89220,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;l  take your advice into consideration. Thank you :-)"},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1629793673,"post_id":41887,"comment_id":89235,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He&#39;s actually 4.5kg right now, so somewhat under the rule of thumb, but the 8 hour sleep seems to be an odd one out, he slept 6 hours last night. Thank you for the information, it helps a lot to stop us worrying!"},{"owner":{"account_id":19170026,"reputation":81,"user_id":39221,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/91aa1adb0e8f50f17add990d43cd8ad6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"buck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39221/buck"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1631814414,"post_id":41929,"comment_id":89352,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He is not very interested in toys. His favorite is pulling my hair. I tried with my hair, it was painful. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1632498115,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89379,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Don&#39;t forget to introduce him to the concept of &quot;straw&quot; at this point, too. It&#39;s very logical to go from &quot;suck on nipple, get liquid&quot; to &quot;suck on straw, get liquid&quot; when the kid&#39;s used to nursing. If you wait until he&#39;s fully used to &quot;tip cup, get liquid&quot; before you introduce the straw, you get a lot of &quot;tip cup, get wet, change clothes&quot;. I&#39;ve seen kids struggle to understand a straw because the parents waited too long."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632501071,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89380,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m not a fan of straws for several reasons, but certainly if you&#39;re going to use straws that&#39;s a great point!"},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632501545,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89381,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;ve seen 2 and 3 year olds not understand that tipping the cup isn&#39;t necessary when using a straw, so unless your &quot;not a fan of&quot; extends to that age (and it&#39;s fine if that&#39;s the case, I&#39;m sure you have your reasons), then it does help to teach &#39;em young."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632501845,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89382,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@FreeMan I&#39;m not a fan of straws at any age, adult included. :)  But absolutely agree with teaching it at this age if you&#39;re not of the same mind as me!"},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632502128,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89383,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I don&#39;t use a sissy stick either, but for those who do... ;)"},{"owner":{"account_id":8066002,"reputation":101,"user_id":26445,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/afab93d01607adc980e2fe3eebdb04a6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"fyrepenguin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26445/fyrepenguin"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1632529812,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89386,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe they also make cups with built-in straws, if the disposable nature is what you consider an issue. They’re honestly pretty neat."},{"owner":{"account_id":1878842,"reputation":111,"user_id":19408,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ad78cf42f8c421bc3e532fc3e16f3f9d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibelas","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/19408/zibelas"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632556079,"post_id":41944,"comment_id":89387,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There are as well a lot of different types of silicon straws that can just be washed, bend and chewed on."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632739700,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89399,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Choking is pretty much the only hazard of acorns aside from simple dirt.  They&#39;re edible once shelled, but the bitter flavour of the tannins would be offputting (a lot of soaking in water followed by light toasting and they can be quite pleasant).  The tannins in the leaves are also presumably unpleasant  if someone tried to eat them, though oak leaves can be made into wine."},{"owner":{"account_id":3700957,"reputation":101,"user_id":41711,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/65b1ece9161b791b5a19d0582e177073?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Stef","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41711/stef"},"edited":false,"score":11,"creation_date":1632739954,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89401,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The fact that oak leaves is used in folk medicine is a surprising argument to support the position that it&#39;s safe for a baby. I would certainly not consider random medicine to be safe for a baby."},{"owner":{"account_id":15983773,"reputation":195,"user_id":41799,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-vy9bO7mC9NU/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACI/s12PH9aZo9E/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Ed HP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41799/ed-hp"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632746602,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89407,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for the well-researched answer.  I&#39;ll likely accept it as the answer, but will first wait the customary 24 hours :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":2672444,"reputation":101,"user_id":7398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ae8474e7af87764563169daf54d4fc82?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dbmag9","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7398/dbmag9"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1632747574,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89408,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"For some interesting research on infants, plants and learning whether they are safe, search for the phrase &quot;To see this system&quot; in this blog post (which is interesting in its entirety) <a href=\"https://slatestarcodex.com/2019/06/04/book-review-the-secret-of-our-success/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">slatestarcodex.com/2019/06/04/&hellip;</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":15983773,"reputation":195,"user_id":41799,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-vy9bO7mC9NU/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACI/s12PH9aZo9E/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Ed HP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41799/ed-hp"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632751826,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89410,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@EdHP Thanks for the comment. No rush at all on accepting the question. I wonder why there are no more answers, though... Feel free to wait as long as you need before accepting - hope you get more answers in the meantime!"},{"owner":{"account_id":15983773,"reputation":195,"user_id":41799,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-vy9bO7mC9NU/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACI/s12PH9aZo9E/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Ed HP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41799/ed-hp"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2672444,"reputation":101,"user_id":7398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ae8474e7af87764563169daf54d4fc82?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dbmag9","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7398/dbmag9"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1632753743,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89412,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@dbmag9 that link is fantastic!  Great to read about how infants learn about and interact with objects from a psychologist&#39;s perspective, as it&#39;s one of things that&#39;s so &quot;obvious&quot; as adults."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3700957,"reputation":101,"user_id":41711,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/65b1ece9161b791b5a19d0582e177073?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Stef","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41711/stef"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1632762066,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89419,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Stef Thank you for your comment! I think that, given the use of oak in folk medicine, and the wide spread of oak trees in general, by now we would have heard reports of adverse reactions from simply <b>touching</b> oak leaves if there were any. Note that I do not say that oak leaves are safe for infants/babies to <b>ingest</b>. That&#39;s a different question. But the OP is only asking about infants/babies touching oak leaves. Please feel free to post any evidence you might find about the adverse effects of touching oak leaves on babies in a comment below, or, better still, in a separate answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632785542,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89422,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;Note that I do not say that oak leaves are safe for infants/babies to ingest.&quot; One implies the other. If it fits in their hands, it&#39;s probably going into their mouth."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632785908,"post_id":41950,"comment_id":89423,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000 I don’t quite see the implication that way. It’s safe for infants to touch a clean floor, but not safe to ingest raw linoleum. Or oak branches for that matter."},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633007124,"post_id":41971,"comment_id":89445,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I will definitely attempt these suggestions once the baby arrives. Thank you for your answer. I know I should not allow them to make me the bad news bear, but I did want to do them (the parents) a favor and wasn&#39;t expecting quite the adverse reaction that I recieved. Thank you for your time and again, will gladly attempt your strategies :) +1"},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633007231,"post_id":41969,"comment_id":89446,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I am sorry to hear that story. It is awfully sad. Thank you for sharing your experiences and letting me know that I am not alone. I will definitely try to have a conversation with her, even if she doesn&#39;t participate in it herself. +1 for your experiences. Thanks again :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633007469,"post_id":41968,"comment_id":89447,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I allowed the older sibling to share her experiences (she&#39;s been there once before) and talked to her about big feelings, its okay to be upset, you aren&#39;t being replaced and there is nothing wrong with your feelings. she still isn&#39;t speaking to me but she sat on the couch next to me and let me hold her blanket. Progress. She still isn&#39;t interacting with her mother but I shared this answer with her as well. I even purchased a book to read to her, which she listened to as I read to her but didn&#39;t speak to me. Thank you for these suggestions, they were truly helpful. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633007563,"post_id":41968,"comment_id":89448,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"and I was also surprised at their request1"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633108416,"post_id":41971,"comment_id":89466,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"From a <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02253287\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Santa Myth study</a> where researchers interviewed kids who had already discovered the truth about Santa: &quot;Children reported predominantly positive reactions on learning the truth. Parents, however, described themselves as predominantly sad in reaction to their child&#39;s discovery.&quot; These are kids whose parents strongly encouraged a belief in Santa. Parenting by lying and manipulation is wrong, and damaging. But about Santa, it&#39;s more about kids developing and flexing the cognitive skills to figure out that it&#39;s a myth."},{"owner":{"account_id":5711979,"reputation":321,"user_id":26750,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a0bcf85ca259d5c6654c758cf2ee4683?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kat","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26750/kat"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633186857,"post_id":41971,"comment_id":89470,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse most people are fine when they learn the truth, but some do consider it a serious betrayal and never forgive it. I&#39;ve heard that from multiple adults myself. It&#39;s definitely a gamble."},{"owner":{"account_id":12906450,"reputation":379,"user_id":39695,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/rcr5R.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Enguroo","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39695/enguroo"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633232033,"post_id":41976,"comment_id":89477,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you for your answer. The first part of it is about sleep in a car seat (I meant my baby wouldn’t sleep well or at all in it while traveling in the car, which is kind of stressful for both me and him because he cries a lot in his seat) and a carrier (from now on, I’ll make sure he doesn’t doze off in it - I didn’t realize that could be so dangerous!) Many thanks 😊"},{"owner":{"account_id":12252380,"reputation":600,"user_id":32209,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6fec1a984ec0320a83126667eff3a0e7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"suse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32209/suse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633235607,"post_id":41929,"comment_id":89478,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I know this is late and hopefully, things have improved by now, but have you tried placing food or treats a few inches in front of him? Also a flashing light or sound-producing toy might stimulate him. Help him by moving the correct limbs and that might jumpstart his interest in moving closer to the object."},{"owner":{"account_id":6229027,"reputation":254,"user_id":26734,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/acYd0.png?s=256","display_name":"Toby Speight","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26734/toby-speight"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1633416943,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89489,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;<i>turn on the AC</i>&quot;?  This is a bedroom in a residential house, not an office with air-conditioning!"},{"owner":{"account_id":1642711,"reputation":101,"user_id":9180,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/QQAJV.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ivo","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9180/ivo"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6229027,"reputation":254,"user_id":26734,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/acYd0.png?s=256","display_name":"Toby Speight","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26734/toby-speight"},"edited":false,"score":14,"creation_date":1633420313,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89490,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TobySpeight in some areas in the world it&#39;s very common to have AC in residential houses"},{"owner":{"account_id":8066002,"reputation":101,"user_id":26445,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/afab93d01607adc980e2fe3eebdb04a6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"fyrepenguin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26445/fyrepenguin"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633447523,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89500,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Or even in apartments. And in some places, many people have separate window unit AC systems, including in bedrooms"},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633448245,"post_id":41985,"comment_id":89501,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;If possible, it&#39;d be good for nap times if you could work in a different location.&quot; OTOH, it&#39;s good for a kid to learn how to fall asleep and stay asleep even in a noisy environment. We <i>never</i> put our kids in a &quot;cone of silence&quot; for sleeping and it paid off when our 6 month old slept through the 2nd half of a basketball playoff game with a dozen people yelling and shouting just outside his bedroom door."},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633448391,"post_id":41985,"comment_id":89502,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;With my three sons, my wife and I had to move each one out of our room by 6 months old because they would wake at night just by us turning over in bed as we slept.&quot; Another benefit to <i>only</i> having the kids sleep in the same room on &quot;special&quot; occasions like nightmares, or when we didn&#39;t have central AC so he slept in the one air conditioned room we had in the house so he didn&#39;t melt into a puddle over night."},{"owner":{"account_id":3231852,"reputation":111,"user_id":16423,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/-KUzcFjFWJxk/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACk/vuVBBRqFkTo/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Nobody","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16423/nobody"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633458598,"post_id":41985,"comment_id":89508,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There is also <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetically_induced_acoustic_noise\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">en.wikipedia.org/wiki/&hellip;</a> and high pitched noises which the parents might not even be able to hear, but which might be painfully loud for a baby. I still remember when I was 18 or 20 or so and was trying to explain in a foreign language to a train conductor that the train was producing a painfully loud noise and the (middle aged) train conductor could barely hear it at all."},{"owner":{"account_id":5263661,"reputation":101,"user_id":41843,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/469837e228452c7a56cf9e28502775d3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Brady Gilg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41843/brady-gilg"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6229027,"reputation":254,"user_id":26734,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/acYd0.png?s=256","display_name":"Toby Speight","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26734/toby-speight"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1633459416,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89510,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TobySpeight Your comment is so shocking to me that I&#39;m curious where you&#39;re from. Certainly here in Arizona, the majority of the population would literally die without AC."},{"owner":{"account_id":1203224,"reputation":556,"user_id":3021,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/BzMXB.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Grant","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3021/grant"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5263661,"reputation":101,"user_id":41843,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/469837e228452c7a56cf9e28502775d3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Brady Gilg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41843/brady-gilg"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633467224,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89514,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@BradyGilg I was curious too. Their profile says Scotland. Apparently a warm summer day in Scotland is around 15-17C (59-63F). Cool enough that AC isn&#39;t really a requirement. Arizona on the other hand has highs around 40C/105F - where yeah, people may literally die in the heat."},{"owner":{"account_id":22651,"reputation":101,"user_id":33491,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/itvGlqzj.png?s=256","display_name":"Don Branson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33491/don-branson"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1642711,"reputation":101,"user_id":9180,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/QQAJV.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ivo","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9180/ivo"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633468158,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89515,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@IvoBeckers In fairness, it&#39;s only common in those few areas closer to the equator than Scotland. ;)"},{"owner":{"account_id":5263661,"reputation":101,"user_id":41843,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/469837e228452c7a56cf9e28502775d3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Brady Gilg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41843/brady-gilg"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1203224,"reputation":556,"user_id":3021,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/BzMXB.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Grant","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3021/grant"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633469445,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89517,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Grant I wish we had highs of 105F. This summer the high was 118F (48C)."},{"owner":{"account_id":6229027,"reputation":254,"user_id":26734,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/acYd0.png?s=256","display_name":"Toby Speight","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26734/toby-speight"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633503837,"post_id":41987,"comment_id":89521,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;ve now looked at OP&#39;s profile, but unfortunately I have no idea whether air-con is normal in Sri Lankan homes, so not sure whether that part of this answer is helpful."},{"owner":{"account_id":85750,"reputation":1992,"user_id":16114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ca29123b5669b26f9b6ca2c62d9d215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Wayne Conrad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16114/wayne-conrad"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633969767,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89568,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The reason we have the scientific method is because things everybody knows can be wrong.  For example, the popular idea that sugar causes hyperactivity is not supported by science.  All that said, I don&#39;t mean to imply that your answer is incorrect-Your arguments seem plausible to me."},{"owner":{"account_id":1234456,"reputation":101,"user_id":38411,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bsxrwWUr.png?s=256","display_name":"alphabetasoup","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38411/alphabetasoup"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633991788,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89570,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The scientific method sits uneasily with such anecdotal evidence because it is often subject to various biases, including in this case confirmation bias. People who think overtiredness exists will tend to observe overtiredness (or at least, will think they are witnessing overtiredness). But they haven&#39;t <i>tested</i> that rigorously. In my own experience (anecdotally), as one who intuits that the idea of &quot;overtiredness&quot; doesn&#39;t make sense, I also don&#39;t think I&#39;ve ever witnessed it. That&#39;s either my bias or my honest observation, but I&#39;m not in a position to discriminate the two."},{"owner":{"account_id":2703317,"reputation":361,"user_id":36231,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4lI4f.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Astralbee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36231/astralbee"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1234456,"reputation":101,"user_id":38411,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bsxrwWUr.png?s=256","display_name":"alphabetasoup","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38411/alphabetasoup"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634051007,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89573,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@alphabetasoup &quot;Overtiredness&quot; is an oversimplified term. If you didn&#39;t appreciate the science behind it, you would rightly ask why being <i>more</i> tired wouldn&#39;t make someone <i>more</i> sleepy. If you read my answer you&#39;ll now know that it <i>does</i> make them more sleepy, but the resulting stress brings other conflicting hormones into play. It would be like an adult drinking very strong coffee because they were extra tired, and then trying to sleep. They <i>are</i> still tired, but now they have an irritant in their system setting their nerves on edge and preventing sleep."},{"owner":{"account_id":2703317,"reputation":361,"user_id":36231,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4lI4f.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Astralbee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36231/astralbee"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":85750,"reputation":1992,"user_id":16114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ca29123b5669b26f9b6ca2c62d9d215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Wayne Conrad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16114/wayne-conrad"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634059593,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89575,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@WayneConrad Sure - the only reason I&#39;ve even talked about the scientific method is that the OP was asking for a scholarly, peer-reviewed paper on this topic. I&#39;m not dismissing it in favour of popular opinion, rather saying that there is no reason to be so sceptical about it when the theory has already been put to the test."},{"owner":{"account_id":1234456,"reputation":101,"user_id":38411,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bsxrwWUr.png?s=256","display_name":"alphabetasoup","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38411/alphabetasoup"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634066698,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89579,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Astralbee I did read your answer. I merely noted that it still amounts to an <i>anecdote</i>, or perhaps &quot;folk wisdom&quot;, and yet my own anecdotal counter-evidence as a father of &quot;three under three&quot; is that I&#39;ve never seen it. We need a better test."},{"owner":{"account_id":2703317,"reputation":361,"user_id":36231,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4lI4f.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Astralbee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36231/astralbee"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1234456,"reputation":101,"user_id":38411,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bsxrwWUr.png?s=256","display_name":"alphabetasoup","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38411/alphabetasoup"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634111635,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89583,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@alphabetasoup I feel my answer &#39;amounted&#39; to more than folk wisdom, as I only addressed that before moving on to the actual science of it. But in light of your higher criticism I have now edited my answer, removing all references to what parents believe and just focused on the actual science of overtiredness."},{"owner":{"account_id":1234456,"reputation":101,"user_id":38411,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bsxrwWUr.png?s=256","display_name":"alphabetasoup","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38411/alphabetasoup"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1634157203,"post_id":42012,"comment_id":89584,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m not attempting to rip your answer apart: it&#39;s plausible, as Wayne Conrad says. It&#39;s just that I&#39;d still like to have a citation to a study, that I can read for my own interest. That would actually answer the question."},{"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634261484,"post_id":42029,"comment_id":89587,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The videos are cocomelon and d billions rythmes."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1634263074,"post_id":42028,"comment_id":89588,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;d upvote the first two paragraphs, but downvote the last one.  Leave personal judgements out of answers, and instead focus on what&#39;s important - answering the question."},{"owner":{"account_id":1312213,"reputation":4224,"user_id":9368,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2442ea911c89187262d6575d739a1ca7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"swbarnes2","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9368/swbarnes2"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1634275357,"post_id":42028,"comment_id":89589,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This poster has multiple questions about how to work around his brother in law being a jerk.  There is a limit to what the OP can do when he&#39;s being sabotaged in his own household."},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634910898,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89662,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We have tried swaddling - we even use the DUDU method linked above. However, getting the bottom part of the swaddle under him is proving to be a challenge as he gets bigger and bigger, and he&#39;s usually wiggled free of his swaddle within 15 minutes of us finishing it - so we aren&#39;t having much luck there."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634911297,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89663,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Zibbobz Swaddling snugly, when done correctly, does not allow the child to wiggle free, certainly not within 15 minutes. Perhaps your swaddle is too loose for the child&#39;s current strength. I would recommend revisiting your swaddling technique. Also, what about the other 4 S&#39;s: Side-Stomach, Shushing, Swinging or Sucking?"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":264}